{"name":"Grapefruit","hex":"#fd5956","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grapefruit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The tree of the species Citrus paradisi, a hybrid of Citrus maxima and sweet orange.","The large spherical tart fruit produced by this tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grapefruit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grapefruit","blocks":[{"text":"The grapefruit is a subtropical citrus tree known for its relatively large, sour to semi-sweet, somewhat bitter fruit. The flesh of the fruit is segmented and varies in color from pale yellow to dark red."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Grapefruits_-_whole-halved-segments.jpg/330px-Grapefruits_-_whole-halved-segments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grapefruit","wikidata":"Q41350"},{"title":"Grapefruit (book)","blocks":[{"text":"Grapefruit is an artist's book written by Yoko Ono, originally published in 1964. It has become famous as an early example of conceptual art, containing a series of \"event scores\" that replace the physical work of art – the traditional stock-in-trade of artists – with instructions that an individual may, or may not, wish to enact."},{"text":"Grapefruit is one of the monuments of conceptual art of the early 1960s. She has a lyrical, poetic dimension that sets her apart from the other conceptual artists. Her approach to art was only made acceptable when [people] like Kosuth and Weiner came in and did virtually the same thing as Yoko, but made them respectable and collectible."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ac/Grapefruit-Ono.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grapefruit_(book)","wikidata":"Q5597012"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg/500px-Grapefruit.ebola.